Presidential elections were held in [[Bulgaria]] on 23 October 2011, with a runoff held on 30 October 2011. Incumbent president [[Georgi Parvanov]] was not eligible for re-election; the [[Constitution of Bulgaria]] limits a president to two terms. No candidate won outright in the first round, resulting in a runoff between the eventual winner, [[Rosen Plevneliev]] of [[GERB]], and [[Ivaylo Kalfin]] of the [[Bulgarian Socialist Party]].<ref>[http://www.businessweek.com/news/2011-10-24/bulgarian-ruling-party-candidate-leads-in-presidential-vote.html Bulgarian ruling party candidate leads in presidential vote] {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111026202711/http://www.businessweek.com/news/2011-10-24/bulgarian-ruling-party-candidate-leads-in-presidential-vote.html |date=October 26, 2011 }} Business Week, 24 October 2011</ref>

==Background==
The presidential elections were held in conjunction with local elections, saving the country BGN 8 million according to the finance minister [[Simeon Djankov]].<ref>{{cite web|title=Bulgaria's '2 in 1' Elections Spared BGN 8 M - FinMin|url=http://www.novinite.com/articles/135899/Bulgaria%27s+%272+in+1%27+Elections+Spared+BGN+8+M+-+FinMin|website=Novinite.com|publisher=Sofia News Agency|accessdate=2 June 2014}}</ref> However, the move drew criticism from the US Ambassador in Sofia, [[James Warlick]], who said that the move led to poor administration during the elections.<ref>{{cite web|title=US Ambassador: Elections in Bulgaria Were Not Administered Well|url=http://www.novinite.com/articles/135607/US+Ambassador%3A+Elections+in+Bulgaria+Were+Not+Administered+Well|website=Novinite.com|publisher=Sofia News Agency|accessdate=2 June 2014}}</ref>

==Candidates==
There were 18 registered candidates.<ref>[http://results.cik.bg/tur1/listi/index.html Candidate lists for President and Vice President of the Republic of Bulgaria] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111026200322/http://results.cik.bg/tur1/listi/index.html |date=2011-10-26 }} Electoral Commission of Bulgaria {{in lang|bg}}</ref> [[Rosen Plevneliev]], [[Ivaylo Kalfin]], and [[Meglena Kuneva]] were expected to have the best chance of reaching the second round.<ref name=euactiv>[http://www.euractiv.com/en/elections/leading-mep-runs-bulgarian-presidency-news-506641 Leading MEP runs for Bulgarian presidency] {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110722044917/http://www.euractiv.com/en/elections/leading-mep-runs-bulgarian-presidency-news-506641 |date=July 22, 2011 }} Eurative, 19 July 2011</ref> The candidates are:<ref>[http://sofiaecho.com/2011/09/18/1158623_gerbs-plevneliev-has-narrow-lead-in-tight-presidential-race-poll GERB's Plevneliev has narrow lead in tight presidential race - poll] The Sofia Echo, 18 September 2011</ref>

