==Conduct==
===MPs interfering with the ballot count===
Two GERB MPs were photographed at the Sofia electoral commission amid the chaos that erupted at the Sofia commission right after the first round of presidential and local elections.<ref name=Interference /> These two MPs were not authorized to be present at the electoral commission at that time: an offence under Bulgarian law. One of the GERB MPs, Stanislav Ivanov, was photographed carrying a large bag around the Sofia Electoral Commission building. This bag was presumably full of ballots.<ref name=Interference>{{cite web|title=Bulgaria Election Body Fails to Censure Ruling Party MPs for Interfering with Elections|url=http://www.novinite.com/articles/133427/Bulgaria+Election+Body+Fails+to+Censure+Ruling+Party+MPs+for+Interfering+with+Elections|work=Novinite.com|publisher=Sofia News Agency|accessdate=20 February 2014}}</ref> The Central Electoral Commission held a meeting on the case but failed to reach a decision, because voting could not reach the required 2/3 majority.<ref name=Interference />

===Universiada Hall crisis===
{{Unreferenced section|date=June 2013}}
Members of electoral committees were held without rest or sleep for 30 to 34 hours.{{Citation needed|reason=no media references|date=June 2013}} Universiada Hall was not aired, was stunningly cold and members of committees were not given food and water, and not allowed to go outside the Hall to buy such.{{Citation needed|reason=no media references|date=June 2013}} Journalists and observers were not allowed in Universiada Hall and a large region around it was slipped off with police until the next day late afternoon and early evening.{{Citation needed|reason=no media references|date=June 2013}}

In the day of the elections and day after one member of Municipal Committee in [[Varna, Bulgaria|Varna]] died, six ambulances arrived at Universiada Hall to assist fainted committee members,{{Citation needed|reason=no media references|date=June 2013}} while the number of fainted man and women, and those suffering health problems during and after elections is not known, according to witnesses people were fainting in the Hall and at the queues for delivering elections protocols, and in the first lines at the committee members meeting in Universiada people were in constant fainted and wakening cycles condition because of lack of air, cold, malnutrition and exhausture.<ref>Video was broadcast in Bulgarian televisions where at the meeting of committee members in Universiada people are scanning 'mafia' because of the conditions [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q4YnSPPaC8k Universiada Hall - BGNES, elections 2011 (video)], [[YouTube]], October 24, 2011. Retrieved 2011-10-26</ref>

People slept on floor, on bags with bulletins{{Citation needed|date=June 2013}} and even some left Universiada Hall unlawfully with taking the bulletins with themselves at home because of the poor conditions.{{Citation needed|reason=no media references|date=June 2013}}

In the first announcement of the situation no politician or Central Committee member expressed any sorry or apology for it and party GERB said that the reason of this was the one-hour delay of the starting of the electoral day but in fact the starting of the electoral day was marked by no voters in the first two hours, while people was arriving at sections to vote around 8 am.{{Citation needed|reason=no media references|date=June 2013}}

After the scandal of the situation of people still at Universiada Hall sparked in media, it was announced that committee members held there will receive 60 lv or 30 Euro more as a compensation for the long hours stay.{{Citation needed|reason=no media references|date=June 2013}}

===International statements===
The [[Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe|PACE]] delegation noted that whilst the election was conducted in a generally orderly and peaceful way, there remained concerns about a lack of an equal access to the media, blurred distinctions between newspaper editorials and political advertisements, the lack of a dedicated voter roll and the candidate registration system which particularly affected independent candidates. PACE also recommended that voting for expatriate Bulgarians should be improved.<ref>[http://sofiaecho.com/2011/10/24/1186262_bulgarias-2011-elections-efficiently-run-but-with-shortcomings-pace Bulgaria’s 2011 elections efficiently run, but with shortcomings – PACE] The Sofia Echo, 24 October 2011</ref> The [[Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe|OCSE]] delegation also noted concerns about the blurred lines in media coverage, as well as vote-buying allegations, restrictions on using minority languages in campaigns, and inflammatory statements by some candidates.<ref>[http://sofiaecho.com/2011/10/25/1186885_osce-positive-about-bulgarian-election-but-worried-by-vote-buying-media-coverage OSCE positive about Bulgarian election but worried by vote-buying, media coverage] The Sofia Echo, 25 October 2011</ref>

